# Per-tenant compose overrides NO LONGER EXIST (one-compose): the
# tracked docker-compose.yml is the ONE file for every tenant, and per-tenant
# differences are values in the tenant env file. This wildcard stays so any STALE
# gitignored docker-compose.<handle>.yml still on a fleet box (pre-cutover leftovers,
# removed at T1.4) can never be committed — and so a customer handle can never leak
# into the public repo. The tracked composes are negated back in below.
# Only docker-compose.yml and the build overlay are tracked.
docker-compose.*.yml
!docker-compose.yml
# The build-only overlay: single, tracked, tenant-agnostic —
# the ONLY place build: stanzas live. Not a per-tenant override (those stay
# ignored above).
!docker-compose.build.yml

# =============================================================================
# Secret-leak hardening (added 2026-06-17 alongside the gitleaks gate)
# =============================================================================
# These are the categories that ACTUALLY leaked into this repo's history (see
# the 2026-06-17 code-health audit). Belt-and-suspenders with the gitleaks gate:
# the gate catches secret SHAPES; .gitignore stops the leak-prone FILE TYPES from
# ever being staged in the first place. ADD-only — nothing above is removed.

# SQL dumps / backups in general (history leaked full table dumps with app rows).
# The migrations stay tracked via the negation that follows.
*.sql
*backup*
!mcp-server/database/migrations/*.sql
# Manual down-migrations (e.g. rollback/046_*_ROLLBACK.sql) live in a subdir so the
# forward-migration runner (which scans migrations/ non-recursively) never auto-runs
# them; they are documented code, not data dumps — keep them tracked.
!mcp-server/database/migrations/rollback/*.sql
# (The better-auth OAuth schema is now standard migration 052 under
# mcp-server/database/migrations/, already un-ignored above by the migrations/*.sql
# negation — no separate un-ignore needed. The old database/better-auth/ path was
# removed when the schema was promoted into the migration runner.)
# Operator runbook SQL (gated, operator-run): parameterized DDL/DML, no table dumps /
# no app rows / no secrets — it's documented code that must ship.
# ...but the backup/offsite SCRIPTS are code, not data dumps — keep them tracked
# (they read secrets at runtime, never embed them; the gitleaks gate enforces that).
# THE NEGATION MUST BE RECURSIVE. The flat `!scripts/backup-*.sh` form covered only
# scripts/ itself, so `*backup*` silently swallowed backup-retention script
# and backup-retention-anchor.test script — the shared retention rule and
# the guard for it — exactly the way it once left the three backup scripts untracked.
# A file git cannot see is a file that does not deploy and that no gate can scan, and
# NOTHING in the tree reports it. backup-retention-anchor.test script
# section F is the check that now does.
# Scoped to *.sh: shell files are code. SQL dumps and archives with "backup" in the
# name stay ignored by the rule above, which is the leak this section is for.
!scripts/**/*backup*.sh
!scripts/**/*offsite*.sh
!scripts/backup-*.sh
!scripts/offsite-*.sh

# Env files — block every variant, but ALWAYS keep the committed example.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

